    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1d5964f2-1d35-54e9-961b-4e572f7a8df2",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-4t}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-4t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-4t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2-4t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+4}-\\sqrt{2-4a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-4a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "94d7f1fa-5077-5344-adc6-76e2af42553a",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
