      ":question/id": "04c4bba6-78ed-5c79-b7a8-0ac2e1299a8d",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-9t}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-9t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-9t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{2-9t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+9}-\\sqrt{2-9a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-9a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{9}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
