      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-14t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-14t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{7}\\sqrt{2-14t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+14}-\\sqrt{2-14a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-14a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
