    "question": {
      ":question/id": "45198a6e-8768-5463-9d02-b52db22eb35b",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{7-z}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{7-z}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{7-z}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{7-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{7-0}-\\sqrt{7-a}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{7}+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{7-a} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{7}+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
