      ":question/id": "8ca25de4-32d4-5614-842f-ad33d773ec48",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{24x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{24x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{24x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|24x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|24x+1|]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|24a+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|24a+1|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot (\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
