      ":question/id": "7744dfdc-12f5-553c-af72-58120403dec2",
      ":question/problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{6/7}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{6/7}-1) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
