    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e081efbe-a5fa-589f-845b-6ff732592a5b",
      ":question/problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[13]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[13]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[13]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{13}{12}{x}^{12/13}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{12/13}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{12/13}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "81a93b1b-e67c-57bf-bbb2-79008c1fb1f5",
