      ":question/id": "39241887-3975-54d5-b3fb-414c9ef3ab6c",
      ":question/problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[17]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[17]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[17]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{17}{16}(2z+1)^{16/17}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{16}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(2z+1)^{16/17}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{16}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }((2a+1)^{16/17}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{16}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
