    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c2cdbfa1-9bea-5767-95fa-60376d585296",
      ":question/problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[21]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[21]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[21]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{21}{20}{x}^{20/21}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{20/21}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{20/21}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{10}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a88143c6-4893-5b0a-90e3-d34d38e8218b",
