    "question": {
      ":question/id": "befa23a4-c3ff-53ea-8cd7-67841fa65b2b",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{15}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 15 & \\infty  \\\\ u & \\operatorname{ln}⁡15 & \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{15}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{15}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}^{a}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡15)] \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡15) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "aa03d7e0-4234-53fc-b137-4fa76bb3e7c7",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
